**Read:** [Static vacuum insulated cryogenic vessels — EIGA 224/20](../sources/Static-Cryogenic-Vessels-EIGA.lingua#r_3RWNED7WZ5GMG7X76ZT96A63HZ), inspection and abnormal conditions; [Thermal performance of cryogenic storage tanks — Aasen and colleagues](../sources/Thermal-Performance-of-Cryogenic-Tanks-Aasen.lingua#r_2GPG0AVVS87R5ZY5Z5GVJEAEN7), thermal model; [Cryogenic vaporisation systems — EIGA 133/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Vaporisation-Systems-EIGA.lingua#r_6CCVCPX8ET2NE6XJ42YW7K4ZDH), cold breakthrough.

An insulating vacuum is part of a thermal resistance network. Its degradation changes gas conduction and can change condensation or deposition on cold surfaces. Heat entering a cryogenic inventory can change phase, pressure and discharge; the inventory's initial state and the connected flow paths determine the transient.

Distinguish an internal process leak, an external air leak and failure of a thermal barrier. Each can change both heat and mass transfer. A steady boil-off estimate does not by itself establish a transient relief load. Frost or pressure history can be evidence, but a single appearance does not uniquely identify the cause.

**Work:** Modify the earlier vessel energy balance to include a time-dependent heat input and phase inventory. Identify which heat-transfer and discharge data a defensible transient calculation would require. Explain why a steady-state insulation rating cannot settle every loss-of-vacuum scenario.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
